---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";
// The preview clones the <head> sheets of the page, but whatever the demo uses has to be
// imported here: `.sk-truncate` is from the demo, not the package.
import "../../../examples/tooltip.css";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/vanilla @skryensya/react`;

/*
 * Three triggers and nothing more: the three cases where a tooltip earns its place, without the dashboard that
 *before it surrounded them. Between one and the other only the shape of the trigger and the placement changes; the rest of the
 * markup is identical, which is just what you need to be able to read.
 *
 * Without layout container: `.sk-tooltip` is `inline-flex`, so the three flow in line where
 * whether they stick. A wrapper here would be markup that is not a tooltip.
 */
const html = `<!-- 1. An icon-only control. The name lives in aria-label; the tooltip expands it. -->
<span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="block-end">
  <button
    class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor"
    type="button"
    data-variant="ghost"
    data-icon-only
    aria-label="Exportar"
    data-sk-anchor-trigger
  ><span data-sk-icon="download"></span></button>
  <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
    <!-- The arrow, optional: is authored or does not exist. It comes out from the trigger, not from the center of the box. -->
    <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Download the visible period in CSV</span>
  </span>
</span>

<!-- 2. Explain a piece of information that is already on the screen: the number is always seen, the tooltip says how it is calculated. -->
<span class="sk-inline" data-gap="xs">
  Ingresos $48.2k
  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor>
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor"
      type="button"
      data-variant="ghost"
      data-icon-only
      data-size="sm"
      aria-label="How Income is Calculated"
      data-sk-anchor-trigger
    ><span data-sk-icon="info"></span></button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Amount invoiced for the period, without taxes or refunds</span>
    </span>
  </span>
</span>

<!-- 3. Return what width had to be cut. The trigger is the truncated text itself. -->
<span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="inline-end">
  <span
    class="sk-truncate sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor"
    tabindex="0"
    data-sk-anchor-trigger
  >Billing pipeline migration</span>
  <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
    <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Billing pipeline migration</span>
  </span>
</span>`;

const react = `import { Tooltip } from "@skryensya/react/tooltip";

// 1. An icon-only control. The name lives in aria-label, the tooltip expands it.
//    \`arrow\` is the same as authoring <span className="sk-anchored-arrow" />
//    inside the positioner in the HTML: optional in both bindings, and off
//    by default.
<Tooltip content="Download the visible period in CSV" placement="block-end" arrow>
  <button aria-label="Exportar" type="button">
    <DownloadIcon />
  </button>
</Tooltip>

// 2. Explain a piece of information that is already on the screen; This tells how it is calculated.
<Tooltip content="Amount invoiced for the period, without taxes or refunds" arrow>
  <button aria-label="How Income is Calculated" type="button">
    <InfoIcon />
  </button>
</Tooltip>

// 3. Return what the width cut off. tabIndex makes text truncated
//    be reachable: without focus, the tooltip would be mouse-only.
<Tooltip content={row.title} placement="inline-end" arrow>
  <span className="sk-truncate" tabIndex={0}>{row.title}</span>
</Tooltip>;`;
---

<Base
  title="Tooltip"
  description="Tooltip: an auxiliary description anchored to the trigger, with aria-describedby, Escape and CSS anchor positioning."
>
  <h1>Tooltip</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Tooltip is an <strong>auxiliary description</strong>, never the name of a control nor the only one
    place where a data lives. The machine hangs <code>aria-describedby</code> from the trigger while
    is open, not <code>aria-labelledby</code>: the control must already have a name accessible by
    your account, and the tooltip expands it.
  </p>

  {/* `menu`: the tooltip paints outside the flow, and in a stage at the top of the content it would not have to
      where to go, so the actual placement would never be seen. */}
  <Showcase html={html} react={react} label="Tooltip" viewport="menu" />

  <h2>The rule that the system cannot verify</h2>
  <p>
    There are two situations with no possible fix within the component. In <strong>touch</strong> there is no
    hover: The machine opens in <code>pointerenter</code> and in <code>focus</code>, so in a
    phone the tooltip practically does not appear. <strong>Without JavaScript</strong> the content is
    It looks hidden and only the machine opens it, so it does not appear either.
  </p>
  <p>
    In both cases, no information is lost <em>because</em> the contract prohibits there being
    information there that is not elsewhere. A tooltip that is the only source of something is a bug.
    who uses it, and the validator cannot detect it: that is why it is written here and in the contract
    <code>@skryensya/core/tooltip</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>WCAG 1.4.13</h2>
  <p>
    The <em>Content on Hover or Focus</em> criterion asks for three things, and all three are met by
    defect. <strong>Disposable</strong>: <kbd class="sk-kbd">Esc</kbd> closes without moving the pointer
    nor the focus. <strong>Persistent</strong>: does not close itself on a timer.
    <strong>Hoverable</strong>: the pointer can reach the tooltip without disappearing.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last one is the machine's <code>interactive</code> option, and comes <strong>on</strong>.
    With it turned off the content receives <code>pointer-events: none</code>, the pointer never reaches and the tooltip closes on the way: that fails the criterion. The temptation is to turn it off reasoning that a descriptive tooltip has nothing to click on, and is a reading error:
    <em>hoverable</em> does not exist to be able to operate the tooltip, it exists to be able to <strong>read it</strong>,
    which is just what someone with screen magnification or shakiness needs. You can
    turn off with <code>data-interactive="false"</code>, and turning it off is knowingly departing from the criteria.
  </p>

  <h2>Placement</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data-sk-placement</code> accepts four values in logical axes:
    <code>block-start</code> (the default), <code>block-end</code>, <code>inline-start</code> and
    <code>inline-end</code>. They are a <em>preference</em>, not a guarantee: if it does not fit, turn to the
    opposite side of the same axis, because whoever orders <code>inline-end</code> wants the tooltip on
    side and falling up would be disobeying, not adapting.
  </p>

  <h2>Posicionamiento</h2>
  <p>
    Where there is <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_anchor_positioning" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CSS anchor positioning</a>,
    The browser places the tooltip: without layout loop and without measuring on each scroll. The enhancer
    prints a unique <code>anchor-name</code> and stops passing inline styles to Zag, so it does not
    There are two positioning engines fighting. Browsers without the API are left with the
    Zag JS positioning, which is the fallback, not an inferior path.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>arrow</strong> follows the same distribution: it comes out of the <em>trigger</em> and not from the center of the
    box, so keep pointing at the control even if the box has moved so as not to go out of
    screen, and flips along with it. It lives inside the positioner and still anchors itself to the trigger,
    because it is <code>fixed</code>: a fix is contained in the viewport, not its parent. In the fallback the
    place the machine. It is counted in <a href="/en/anchoring">Anchor</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
</Base>
